#Rigid-group definitions for RNA, for use WITH the bonded force field: the
#same articulated backbone as RNAAtomRigidGroups.rbody, but the nucleobase is
#one group per atom instead of one rigid clique.
#
#REQUIRES -bondedinteraction with RNAAtomTorsion.bi.ff and -dihedralplanarity
#(or -dihedral). Without them the split base has nothing holding its plane and
#folds; RNAAtomRigidGroups.rbody is the springs-only file and stays as it is,
#because 078 builds the 70S from it with no bonded term at all.
#
#WHY SPLIT IT. A rigid group springs every pair it lists, so a 15-atom adenine
#base clique creates 105 springs where AMBER has 16 bonds and 24 valence
#angles. Measured on 075's hairpin, the 65 surplus springs per base -- 735 of
#the network's 1909 -- cost:
#  - the exocyclic amine's rotation: a full-turn barrier of 2838 kJ/mol against
#    AMBER's own 77, i.e. 36.6x, with its minimum displaced by 165 deg. That is
#    the whole of the "amine hydrogens 90 deg out of their base plane" defect:
#    the right table is there and the mesh outvotes it.
#  - global deformability: for the deformations AMBER prices under 1000 kJ/mol
#    the clique asks 1.7 to 4.1 times AMBER's energy, the split base 0.74 to
#    0.89 (0.25 A RMSD, rest lengths re-zeroed, bonded terms only).
#and buy nothing: at 310 K the base's own ring stays as flat with the split as
#with the clique -- RMS deviation from its best-fit plane 0.041 A against
#0.041, where AMBER's own trajectory gives 0.043. The PLANARITY impropers and
#the ring-bond torsions of RNAAtomTorsion.bi.ff do that job, and they are
#exactly what the DNA file's header said to wait for before splitting.
#
#The split makes the spring set EQUAL to AMBER's exclusion set, exactly.
#Measured against amber14/RNA.OL3 on 075: AMBER excludes 1165 pairs (its 1-2
#and 1-3) and SCALES 983 1-4 pairs. The split mesh springs 1165 of those 1165
#and none of the 983. The clique springs the same 1165 -- and 293 of the 983
#as well, a third of AMBER's 1-4 pairs, all of them inside the bases.
#
#That matters because BioSpring excludes only SPRUNG pairs from steric and
#Coulomb, and scales nothing: its 1-4 pairs are live at full strength
#everywhere. The clique was quietly exempting the bases from that, so the
#split does not create the 1-4 problem -- it stops hiding it. The cost of
#what it stops hiding, on this hairpin: +289 kJ/mol of Lennard-Jones (AMBER
#charges +289 on the same 293 pairs, so the error is exactly the missing
#factor of two), every base ring 14 pm wider than AMBER's (1.384 A against
#1.371) and the glycosidic torsion dragged 91 deg off its start against
#AMBER's own 27. Give the heavy-atom bonds AMBER's own stiffness and the ring
#error falls to 3 pm, since the uniform 500 kJ/mol/A2 is 5.7 times too soft to
#hold a ring against that pressure -- the two defects are coupled.
#
#So the examples do not switch to this file yet: a 1-4 scale factor in the
#non-bonded terms is the prerequisite, and it is worth having for the whole
#model, not just for the bases.
#
#Companion file for DNA: DNAAtomRigidGroups.rbody, whose header carries the
#full derivation of the group/hinge layout. Only what differs for RNA is
#repeated here.
#
#SINGLE STRAND. Nothing in this file couples two chains -- the format has no
#notion of chain, +/- only walk the sequence. That is the natural fit for RNA,
#which is normally one strand; its own secondary structure (hairpins, stems)
#is base pairing, so it must come from the hydrogen-bond mechanism
#(biospring's hbond.* settings and a .hbond donor/acceptor table), never from
#a rigid-body rule. A stem-loop built with this file and no hbond term will
#simply unfold into an open strand.
#
#EVERY RULE IS WRITTEN TWICE, once under amber99sb.xml's residue names
#(RA/RC/RG/RU) and once under A/C/G/U. Both spellings are in real use:
#amber99sb.xml says RA, while RNA.OL3.xml -- the reference RNA force field,
#and the one these parameters should come from -- says A, as does any modern
#PDB file. A rule knowing only one spelling matches NOTHING on a file
#written in the other, with no error: measured, 0 springs out of 963
#particles on a real rRNA fragment. Same silent-mismatch class as the sugar
#hydrogen names below. Keep both copies in step when editing.
#
#TWO DIFFERENCES FROM DNA, both on C2':
#  - the 2'-OH replaces one of the two 2' hydrogens, so _C2 lists O2' where
#    the DNA file lists H2'2. Atom names follow amber99sb.xml: O2' and HO'2
#    (the hydroxyl hydrogen is HO'2, not H2'2 -- easy to misread).
#  - that hydroxyl is a real rotor, so it gets its own _O2 group overlapping
#    _C2 on exactly {C2', O2'}. This is the same construction as Ser/Thr/Tyr
#    hydroxyls in ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody.
#
#The 2'-OH is also why RNA sits overwhelmingly in the C3'-endo (North)
#pucker where B-DNA sits in C2'-endo: the substituent disfavours the South
#basin sterically and electronically. This file does not encode that
#preference, and it does not leave the pucker free either: see the DNA
#file's header for why a five-membered ring cannot be freed by per-vertex
#groups (measured 10/10 -- fixing bonds and angles rigidifies a 5-ring).
#The sugar is therefore rigid here too, frozen at the input structure's
#pucker. Reproducing a genuine bimodal North/South distribution is the job
#of the bonded parameters, and for RNA it is the single most important
#thing to validate once they exist.

#Adenine ------------------------------------------------------------------
A_P    RA  -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
A_P    A   -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
A_O5   RA  P O5' C5'
A_O5   A   P O5' C5'
A_C5   RA  O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
A_C5   A   O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
A_C4   RA  C5' C4' H4' O4' C3'
A_C4   A   C5' C4' H4' O4' C3'
A_O4   RA  C4' O4' C1'
A_O4   A   C4' O4' C1'
A_C1   RA  O4' C1' H1' C2' N9
A_C1   A   O4' C1' H1' C2' N9
A_C2   RA  C1' C2' H2'1 H2' O2' C3'
A_C2   A   C1' C2' H2'1 H2' O2' C3'
A_O2   RA  C2' O2' HO'2 HO2'
A_O2   A   C2' O2' HO'2 HO2'
A_C3   RA  C2' C3' H3' C4' O3'
A_C3   A   C2' C3' H3' C4' O3'
A_O3   RA  C3' O3' +P
A_O3   A   C3' O3' +P
A_BN9   RA  N9 C1' C4 C8
A_BC8   RA  C8 H8 N7 N9
A_BN7   RA  N7 C5 C8
A_BC5   RA  C5 C4 C6 N7
A_BC6   RA  C6 C5 N1 N6
A_BN6   RA  N6 C6 H61 H62
A_BN1   RA  N1 C2 C6
A_BC2   RA  C2 H2 N1 N3
A_BN3   RA  N3 C2 C4
A_BC4   RA  C4 C5 N3 N9
A_BN9   A   N9 C1' C4 C8
A_BC8   A   C8 H8 N7 N9
A_BN7   A   N7 C5 C8
A_BC5   A   C5 C4 C6 N7
A_BC6   A   C6 C5 N1 N6
A_BN6   A   N6 C6 H61 H62
A_BN1   A   N1 C2 C6
A_BC2   A   C2 H2 N1 N3
A_BN3   A   N3 C2 C4
A_BC4   A   C4 C5 N3 N9
A_5TER RA  H5T HO5' O5' C5'
A_5TER A   H5T HO5' O5' C5'
A_3TER RA  C3' O3' H3T HO3'
A_3TER A   C3' O3' H3T HO3'

#Cytosine -----------------------------------------------------------------
C_P    RC  -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
C_P    C   -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
C_O5   RC  P O5' C5'
C_O5   C   P O5' C5'
C_C5   RC  O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
